  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 230 PM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday: \n\nThe warm and unsettled weather is expected to continue across \nsouthern Wisconsin through the extended. With an upper level high \ncentered over the southern CONUS, south to southwest winds straight \nout of the Gulf will remain through the extended. Therefore, \ntemperatures in the 80s and dewpoints in the upper 60s to 70s are \nlikely to continue. With that the high heat index is likely to \npersist as well. With the sustained southwesterly flow, there will \nbe enough warm air and moisture for storms at any given time through \nthe extended. For Friday, there will be the combination of the LLJ \nduring the overnight hours with a lingering sfc boundary for day \ntime development. Similar to what we are seeing earlier in the week. \n\nOnce we get into the weekend the diurnal instability remains, \nbut there is a noticeable lack of sfc forcing. The LLJ shifts \nnorth and west while the sfc frontal boundary sags to the south.\nThis will lead to more disorganized rain and storms chances. \nShear looks to weaken as well during this time which in turn \nlimits the potential for an pesky isolated stronger storms. \nRealistically it should close the door on it, but if there is \nenough diurnally driven instability then we don't want to count\nout any single cell pop up thunderstorm just yet. A \nthunderstorm that gets tall enough or water logged enough can \nalways cause some downburst type winds. But that should be very \nfew and far between, more an exception that a rule in the \nenvironment we should have. \n\nLooking further ahead into early next week there is a single aloft \nthat the ridge will weaken a bit and get undercut as a low pressure \nsystem swings through Canada. If these trends continue its possible \nthat as the sfc low moves through the Hudson Bay area that a cold \nfront could move across the Great Lakes Region. All this is to say \nis there is a signal for more organized rain/storms. The GFS by far \nis much further south in comparison to the EURO and CAN run \n(deterministic). With the uncertainty in the track and strength of \nthe low, we could end up with more of the same from the weekend \n(unstable, hot and moist with no forcing). Something to keep an eye \non, but nothing of large concern at this point.",
